-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Template: the view `allusers` with account deactivation (U-127).
--
-- For the configuration management that owns the view (Salt in the reference
-- installation). mailadmin itself never creates or changes a view — its
-- migrator refuses view definitions on principle (E-1, AdditiveOnlyRule).
--
-- MANDATORY ORDER
--
--   1. Run mailadmin's migrations:   php bin/migrate.php
--      This creates `mailadmin_account_status` (migration 0060).
--   2. Check:                        php bin/db-check.php
--      "Account status (U-127)" must list the table as present.
--   3. Only then roll out this view.
--   4. Check again:                  php bin/db-check.php
--      allusers must read "honours the status table in every UNION branch".
--
-- Rolling out the view on a database WITHOUT the table breaks every login on
-- the server: MariaDB refuses to create the view, and a view whose table
-- disappears later fails every query pam_mysql sends — IMAP, POP, ManageSieve
-- and SMTP-AUTH alike. The reverse is harmless: the old view next to the new
-- table simply ignores the table, and mailadmin refuses to disable an account
-- until the view honours it (`account.status_not_enforced`).
--
-- ROLLBACK
--
-- Re-create the previous definition. Every disabled account can log in again at
-- once; nothing else changes. Leave the table where it is.
--
-- ADAPT, DO NOT COPY BLINDLY
--
-- The statement below is mailadmin's reference reconstruction of `allusers`
-- (docker/db/init/10-views.sql), extended by the status filter. The production
-- definition may differ in detail — read it first (`SHOW CREATE VIEW
-- allusers`) and add exactly the two things below to EACH branch of its UNION,
-- changing nothing else:
--
--   LEFT JOIN mailadmin_account_status
--          ON mailadmin_account_status.username = accountuser.username
--   WHERE mailadmin_account_status.username IS NULL
--      OR mailadmin_account_status.disabled = 0
--
-- The join key is `accountuser.username` as stored — not the derived address of
-- the first branch.
--
-- PRIVILEGES
--
-- With the default `SQL SECURITY DEFINER`, the view's definer needs SELECT on
-- `mailadmin_account_status`; the account pam_mysql connects with needs nothing
-- new. With `SQL SECURITY INVOKER`, that account needs
--
--   GRANT SELECT ON <database>.mailadmin_account_status TO '<pam user>'@'<host>';
--
-- WHAT IT DOES AND DOES NOT DO
--
-- A disabled account is absent from the view, so saslauthd/pam_mysql refuses
-- its credentials on every service that authenticates through it. Mail is still
-- delivered to the mailbox (Postfix reads `virtual`, not this view). Sessions
-- that are already open stay open until the client disconnects, and a
-- credential cache in saslauthd (`-c`) keeps answering "yes" until its entries
-- expire. See docs/features/A2-konten.md, "Deaktivierung".
--
-- This file must stay identical to the statement in docker/db/init/10-views.sql;
-- tests/Integration/TestEnvironment/AccountStatusViewTest.php checks it.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

CREATE OR REPLACE VIEW allusers AS
SELECT CONCAT(REPLACE(accountuser.username, CONCAT('.', accountuser.domain_name), ''), '@', accountuser.domain_name) AS username,
       accountuser.password                                                                                          AS password,
       accountuser.domain_name                                                                                       AS domain_name,
       accountuser.imap                                                                                              AS imap,
       accountuser.pop                                                                                               AS pop,
       accountuser.sieve                                                                                             AS sieve,
       accountuser.smtpauth                                                                                          AS smtpauth
FROM accountuser
         LEFT JOIN mailadmin_account_status ON mailadmin_account_status.username = accountuser.username
WHERE mailadmin_account_status.username IS NULL
   OR mailadmin_account_status.disabled = 0
UNION
SELECT accountuser.username    AS username,
       accountuser.password    AS password,
       accountuser.domain_name AS domain_name,
       accountuser.imap        AS imap,
       accountuser.pop         AS pop,
       accountuser.sieve       AS sieve,
       accountuser.smtpauth    AS smtpauth
FROM accountuser
         LEFT JOIN mailadmin_account_status ON mailadmin_account_status.username = accountuser.username
WHERE mailadmin_account_status.username IS NULL
   OR mailadmin_account_status.disabled = 0;
